---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

export const prerender = true;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial Team ｜ PubMed Trivia"
  description="The PubMed Trivia editorial team is responsible for editorial policy, prompt design, NG-term list maintenance, article sampling review, and error-correction response. Transparent process disclosure."
  lang="en"
>
  <article class="editor-page">
    <h1>📝 PubMed Trivia Editorial Team</h1>
    <p class="editor-page__lede">
      Our goal is to deliver primary scientific literature to general readers both accurately and accessibly.
      The editorial team uses Claude (Anthropic) as an adaptation-assistant tool while publishing articles.
    </p>

    <h2>Editorial Responsibility</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Site operation and editorial responsibility: PubMed Trivia Editorial Team</strong></li>
      <li>Contact: <a href="mailto:sciencepubmedjp@gmail.com">sciencepubmedjp@gmail.com</a></li>
      <li>Response time: within 48 hours (excluding weekends/holidays)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Editorial Approach</h2>
    <p>
      PubMed Trivia is <strong>not simply "a site that lets AI summarize papers."</strong>
      Our editorial team designs, maintains, and reviews each step of the following workflow:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Paper selection query design</strong>: The editorial team defines PubMed E-utilities search queries per category (psychology/biology).
        Case reports, retracted papers, and opinion pieces are excluded; systematic reviews, meta-analyses, and comprehensive reviews are prioritized.</li>
      <li><strong>Adaptation prompt design</strong>: The system prompt passed to Claude is written and maintained by the editorial team.
        Three strict constraints are enforced: "Do not deviate from the original abstract's facts," "correlation ≠ causation," and
        "no promotional language that would violate medical-claims law (薬機法)."</li>
      <li><strong>NG-term list maintenance</strong>: Terms like "guaranteed to cure," "lose weight," or "prevent disease" that would violate
        pharmaceutical-affairs or representation-of-prizes law are listed, and output containing them triggers regeneration.</li>
      <li><strong>Sampling review</strong>: Regular random sampling of published articles to check for misreading of the source, grey-area medical claims,
        and naturalness of translation. Problems trigger immediate correction.</li>
      <li><strong>Error-correction response</strong>: Reader reports are verified within 48 hours; articles are corrected if needed and dateModified is updated.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Claude (AI) — Role and Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      Article adaptation uses Anthropic's <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/claude" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Claude Sonnet 4.5</a> and Haiku 4.5.
      AI is used strictly as <strong>"an assistant for adapting original-abstract facts into Japanese/English prose,"</strong>
      following these principles:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>AI output is <strong>always published with the PMID and source URL side-by-side</strong>, so readers can verify primary sources.</li>
      <li>The prompt systematically suppresses the AI's tendency to upgrade "correlation" to "causation."</li>
      <li>The prompt explicitly forbids using any information outside the original abstract, to prevent hallucinated facts or numbers.</li>
      <li>Even so, we cannot guarantee zero AI errors, which is why we <strong>welcome reader error reports</strong> and commit to responding within 48 hours.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Ongoing Article-Quality Improvements</h2>
    <p>
      Published articles continue to receive editorial-quality improvements. See <a href="/en/editorial-policy/">Editorial Policy → Update Log</a> for details.
      For example, on 2026-10-11 the editorial team added four analytical sections (Background, Mechanism, Limitations, Reader implications)
      to 297 of 625 articles, bringing every article to over 2,000 characters.
    </p>

    <h2>Intended Audience</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Undergraduate and graduate students (as an entry point for thesis-related literature search)</li>
      <li>Researchers and healthcare professionals (for catching up on fields outside one's specialty)</li>
      <li>Science-interested general readers (who want to verify the primary source behind "latest research" news items)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/en/editorial-policy/">Editorial Policy</a></li>
      <li><a href="/en/about/">About this site</a></li>
      <li><a href="/en/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a></li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <style>
    .editor-page { max-width: 820px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 24px 20px 60px; }
    .editor-page h1 { font-size: 1.9rem; color: var(--navy); margin: 0 0 12px; }
    .editor-page__lede { font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.85; color: #2d3748; margin: 0 0 24px; background: #f0f4fa; padding: 14px 18px; border-left: 4px solid var(--navy); border-radius: 4px; }
    .editor-page h2 { font-size: 1.25rem; color: var(--navy); margin: 32px 0 12px; padding-left: 12px; border-left: 5px solid var(--navy); }
    .editor-page p { line-height: 1.85; color: #2d3748; margin: 0 0 14px; }
    .editor-page ul, .editor-page ol { line-height: 1.9; color: #2d3748; padding-left: 24px; }
    .editor-page a { color: var(--link); font-weight: 500; }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
